# THE M4 MILESTONE GATE, as a re-runnable script.
#
#   python3 -m http.server 8000 --directory web/dist-m1 &
#   node web/tools/drive-browser.mjs --headed --out /tmp/mile-chrome \
#        --script-file web/tools/persistence-milestone-gate.steps
#   node web/tools/drive-firefox.mjs           --out /tmp/mile-firefox \
#        --script-file web/tools/persistence-milestone-gate.steps
#   node docs/evidence/m4-persistence/check-milestone-transcript.mjs \
#        /tmp/mile-chrome/console.log
#   kill %1
#
# --headed is REQUIRED for Chrome. This script is made of key presses, and
# headless Chrome 152 floods the page with thousands of spurious keydown events
# per real one; see the header of web/tools/drive-browser.mjs. Firefox headless
# is fine.
#
# WHAT THIS ASSERTS THAT THE THREE TASK GATES DO NOT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three gates already in docs/evidence/ each prove one mechanism:
# m4-persist that /persist is IndexedDB-backed and populated before main();
# m4-persist-settings that leaving a menu saves; m4-config-precedence that
# autoexec.cfg no longer overrides the player. None of them asserts the
# MILESTONE's claim, which is about the player's experience across a reload and
# not about any one mechanism. This file asserts exactly that, in three parts:
#
#   A1  THE RESOLUTION THE PLAYER CHOSE IS THE SIZE THE GAME COMES BACK AT.
#       Measured on document.getElementById('canvas').width/height, which is
#       outside the wasm entirely -- no config file is consulted to establish
#       it. The page's own HTML says width="1024" height="768"
#       (web/shell.html), so on every boot the canvas STARTS at 1024x768 and
#       only becomes 320x200 because the game read the choice back and called
#       SDL_SetVideoMode with it. That before/after pair inside one page load
#       is the whole of A1: it cannot be satisfied by the page, by the
#       browser, or by anything that did not go through st_LoadConfig.
#
#   A2  THE BOOT AFTER THE FIRST ONE SKIPS THE FIRST-USE PATH.
#       This is the assertion that catches the failure mode M4 task 1 was
#       built to make unrepresentable, and it is worth stating why it is not
#       redundant with that task's own gate. FS.syncfs(true, cb) is
#       ASYNCHRONOUS. If the populate is ever not awaited -- a future edit to
#       the run dependency in web/shell.html's preRun, say -- then
#       st_LoadConfig reads an empty /persist and the first st_SaveConfig
#       writes a fresh file over the top. SAVING KEEPS WORKING. Every
#       [PERSISTSAVE] line still appears, the file still lands in IndexedDB,
#       and from inside the game the failure is indistinguishable from
#       success. What changes is that every boot is a FIRST boot. So A2 is
#       checked on the game's behaviour, not on the save path.
#
#   A3  THE GAME STILL STEERS, WHICH IS THE ONLY TEST OF THE KEYCODE ROUND
#       TRIP ANYWHERE IN THIS PROJECT.
#       src/tools/tConfiguration.cpp loads default.cfg only while st_FirstUse
#       is true, and gArmagetron.cpp's sg_StartupPlayerMenu applies a
#       keys_*.cfg template only on that same path. From boot 2 onward
#       user.cfg is therefore the SOLE source of every key binding in the
#       game. And the encoding changes in transit: keys_cursor.cfg spells the
#       left arrow 276 (SDL 1.2), su_TranslateSDL12Keysym in src/ui/uInput.cpp
#       re-encodes it to 1104 (SDL 2, as Emscripten's shim delivers it), and
#       tConfItem_key::WriteVal writes back whatever is in keymap[], i.e. the
#       NEW number. uInput.cpp's comment on that function claims the
#       translation is idempotent and therefore "survives M4's user.cfg round
#       trip". That claim has never been tested. This is the test.
#
# HOW A3 IS OBSERVED, AND WHY NOT WITH A SCREENSHOT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "The cycle turned" is easy to see and hard to assert. Three candidates were
# considered and rejected before the one below was found:
#
#   * PIXELS. A 32x24 grayscale thumbnail of the canvas sampled at 10 Hz,
#     scored as mean absolute difference between consecutive frames. A turn
#     yaws the chase camera ~90 degrees and does produce a spike -- but so
#     does a round boundary, an explosion and a menu opening, and those alone
#     already occupy the range a turn would have to be picked out of: in
#     reconnaissance the series reached 23.9 in a window where no key was
#     pressed at all, and 31.6 in one where a round boundary landed. Telling a
#     turn from that needs a tuned threshold, which is not an observation.
#   * DEATH_SUICIDE. Steering into your own wall is logged (gCycle.cpp,
#     sg_deathSuicideWriter). Rejected for a reason that is about the SIGNAL
#     and not about the odds: it is not a signature of turning at all.
#     gCycle::KillAt attributes a death to the player themself whenever no
#     enemy influenced them recently, so driving straight into the rim is a
#     suicide too -- the event says "nobody else killed you", not "you turned".
#     It is also one-shot and geometry-dependent, and the human's lifetime is
#     not fixed: across five reconnaissance rounds, NEW_ROUND to death measured
#     6.5 s four times and 9.4 s once (and DEATH_FRAG all five times, never
#     DEATH_SUICIDE, in rounds where no key press landed while the cycle was
#     alive). The counter below has neither problem: it registers the turn
#     ITSELF, whether or not the cycle then dies.
#   * THE TUTORIAL MATCH. Gentle enough to steer in comfortably -- and
#     unreachable, because welcome() only runs it while st_FirstUse is true,
#     which is precisely the boot A3 is not about.
#
# WHAT IS USED INSTEAD IS THE GAME'S OWN TOOLTIP COUNTER, and it is exact.
# uActionTooltip (src/ui/uInput.h) is a tConfItemBase holding one
# activations-left count per player. config/default.cfg ships
# CYCLE_TURN_LEFT_TOOLTIP as "0 2 1 1 1" and CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT_TOOLTIP as
# "0 3 1 1 1" -- 2 and 3 for player 1. uBindPlayer::DoActivate decrements it,
# and ONLY when the action was actually accepted:
#
#     ret = uPlayerPrototype::PlayerConfig(ePlayer-1)->Act(act,x);
#     if( ret && act && act->GetTooltip() && x > 0 )
#         act->GetTooltip()->Count(ePlayer);
#
# So a decrement means a key press reached keymap[], resolved to
# CYCLE_TURN_LEFT for player 1, and was accepted and executed by the player's
# CYCLE OBJECT. It is written back to user.cfg by uActionTooltip::WriteVal, so
# it is readable from JavaScript. That makes A3 a comparison of two integers
# rather than a judgement about a picture -- and it fails, rather than
# degrading, if the binding did not survive: with no bind on the arrow's keysym
# there is no activation, no decrement, and the counter is still 2.
#
# IT DOES NOT MEAN THE CYCLE WAS ALIVE, and an earlier version of this comment
# said it did. gCycle::Act has no aliveness guard on its turn arms -- its only
# Alive() reference is server-only and does not return, and the next statement
# turns and returns true -- and gCycle::Kill opens with "keep this cycle alive"
# and never clears ePlayerNetID::object, so a press landing after the player
# died still counts. Three things ARE established, and together they are the
# whole of the round trip A3 exists to test: the press resolved through keymap[]
# to the right action for the right player; the CAMERA did not take it, because
# eCamera::Act sends both turn actions to its trailing `else return false;`, so
# DoActivate's `ret` can only have come from the object arm of ePlayer::Act; and
# the player had a cycle object in a running round whose gCycle::Act cleared the
# premature-input guard and called Turn(). A press during the countdown returns
# false at that guard, so a mistimed run FAILS this rather than passing weakly.
#
# ZERO HAS A SECOND PRODUCER, and the route below stays away from it.
# uActionTooltip::Disable zeroes the counter for every bound action at once,
# with no activation involved, and it is called from s_InputConfigGeneric --
# that is, on opening ANY input-configuration menu. Nothing here enters Player
# Setup. A future edit that walked through the bindings screen would satisfy
# these checks with no steering at all.
#
# THE COUNTER IS ALSO WHAT MAKES A2's STRONGEST CHECK POSSIBLE, and the LOAD
# ORDER is what makes that airtight rather than merely persuasive.
# st_LoadConfig loads var/user.cfg FIRST and only then reaches
# `if (st_FirstUse) Load( config, "default.cfg" )`, so a boot that re-ran first
# use would have default.cfg OVERWRITE the spent values it had just read. So a
# boot that has spent the counter down to 0, reloaded, and SAVES 0 again from
# its own memory has demonstrated that default.cfg was not re-read -- i.e.
# that the first-use path was skipped -- from inside the running program,
# rather than by reading FIRST_USE out of a file the game might have ignored.
#
# WHY THE RESOLUTION IS CHOSEN BY CLAMPING RATHER THAN BY COUNTING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# uMenuItemSelection::LeftRight (src/ui/uMenu.h) CLAMPS at both ends -- it does
# not wrap. The Screen Resolution list is built by gResMenEntry from the fixed
# table in rScreen.cpp, sorted ascending by rScreenSize::Compare with the
# 0x0 "desktop" entry last (Compare treats width==0 as greater than
# everything). The game boots on that desktop entry, so it starts at the TOP
# of the list, and pressing Left twenty times lands on the FIRST entry --
# 320x200 -- for any number of presses from thirteen upward. A run that loses
# a keystroke to a slow frame therefore still selects exactly the same
# resolution, which is what makes this gate re-runnable rather than
# occasionally landing one row off and reporting a failure that is about the
# harness.
#
# 320x200 is also the only entry in that list that collides with nothing else
# in the file: it is not 1024x768 (the canvas's HTML default, and the desktop
# size Emscripten reports), and not 640x480 (ARMAGETRON_WINDOWSIZE's own
# default, which would be indistinguishable if FULLSCREEN ever flipped).
#
# WHAT GETS SAVED IS THE ROW, NOT THE PIXELS, and the transcript shows it:
# ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE comes back as 14, which is ArmageTron_Custom, NOT the
# 1 of ArmageTron_320_200. That is not a mis-selection. config/settings_visual
# .cfg ships CUSTOM_SCREEN_WIDTH 320 and CUSTOM_SCREEN_HEIGHT 200, so the
# custom row IS 320x200 here; rScreenSize::Compare deliberately ignores the
# res enum ("res is not really a criterion"), so the two rows are equal and
# the std::set keeps the one inserted first -- and gResMenEntry's loop runs
# from ArmageTron_Custom downward, so that is the custom one.
#
# The distinction is worth stating because it is what A1 actually rides on.
# ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE_W and _H are saved too, but lowlevel_sr_InitDisplay
# calls res.UpdateSize() before using them, which overwrites both from the
# compiled table indexed by the enum -- and for row 14 that table's entries
# are the CUSTOM_SCREEN_* settings, which are tSettingItem and therefore never
# written to user.cfg at all. So the quantity that crosses the reload is the
# player's CHOICE OF ROW, and the pixel count is re-derived from it on the
# other side. That is exactly what "the game remembered what I picked" means,
# and it is stable across runs because config/settings.cfg -- which includes
# settings_visual.cfg -- is loaded on every boot, not only the first
# (src/tools/tConfiguration.cpp, st_LoadConfig: only default.cfg is gated on
# st_FirstUse).
#
# NOTHING HERE PRESSES "APPLY CHANGES", AND THAT IS THE POINT. sr_ReinitDisplay
# is never called, so boot 2's canvas stays 1024x768 with the choice made --
# recorded, so it can be checked rather than asserted. The only thing that ever
# resizes the canvas in this run is boot 3's own startup. The menu says as much
# on screen: "Changes will be applied the next time you start Armagetron
# Advanced or when you select Apply Changes below."
#
# THE VIEWPORT CONFIGURATION SCREEN IS NOT ON THIS ROUTE. rViewport.cpp holds a
# latent abort in this port; it is reached from PLAYER SETUP, not from Display
# Settings (M4 task 3 established that, correcting task 2's belief). Nothing
# below enters Player Setup.
#
# THE TRANSCRIPT IS CUMULATIVE across three page loads. The marks
# RELOAD-1-REQUESTED and RELOAD-2-REQUESTED partition it. Every payload also
# carries its own "phase" string, so the checker never infers which boot a line
# came from by counting.
#
# ONE THING THIS GATE DOES NOT SEPARATE, stated so nobody claims otherwise: it
# does not attribute the saves to the menu-leave callback rather than to the
# beforeunload backstop. FIRST_USE 0 in particular reaches the file through the
# backstop, because gArmagetron.cpp flips st_FirstUse in a gap no menu exit can
# see (docs/evidence/m4-persist-settings/README.md has the measurement). That
# separation is task 2's gate's job and it does it with a control build. This
# gate is about the milestone's outcome, and both mechanisms are part of it.

# ===========================================================================
# BOOT 1 -- a first-time visitor, on a fresh browser profile.
# ===========================================================================

until:1:90000:[PERSIST] populate ok
mark:BOOT-1-POPULATED
wait:1000

eval:(()=>{const S={};S.rd=(p)=>{try{return Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){return null}};S.line=(t,k)=>{if(t===null)return null;const m=t.split('\n').find(l=>{const s=l.trim();return s.indexOf(k)===0&&/\s/.test(s.charAt(k.length))});return m?m.trim().slice(k.length).trim().replace(/\s+/g,' '):null};S.keys=(t)=>t===null?[]:t.split('\n').filter(l=>l.trim().indexOf('KEYBOARD')===0).map(l=>l.trim().replace(/\s+/g,' '));S.binds=(K,a)=>K.filter(l=>l.indexOf(' PLAYER_BIND '+a+' 1')>0).map(l=>l.split(' ')[1]);S.dump=(phase)=>{const c=S.rd('/persist/var/user.cfg');const cv=document.getElementById('canvas');const K=S.keys(c);console.log('[MILE] '+JSON.stringify({phase:phase,canvas_w:cv.width,canvas_h:cv.height,present:c!==null,bytes:c===null?null:c.length,first_use:S.line(c,'FIRST_USE'),fullscreen:S.line(c,'FULLSCREEN'),screenmode:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE'),screenmode_w:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE_W'),screenmode_h:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE_H'),windowsize_w:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_WINDOWSIZE_W'),windowsize_h:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_WINDOWSIZE_H'),n_keyboard:K.length,left_binds:S.binds(K,'CYCLE_TURN_LEFT'),right_binds:S.binds(K,'CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT'),tip_left:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_LEFT_TOOLTIP'),tip_right:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT_TOOLTIP')}));return phase};window.__mile=S;return 'probe installed'})()

# A fresh temp profile per run, so IndexedDB starts empty and there is no
# user.cfg at all. This is what makes every later "it was read back" a fact
# about this run rather than about a leftover profile.
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot1-before-play')

click:#start
wait:9000
shot:00-boot1-language-menu

# Enter accepts the highlighted language AND leaves the language menu.
key:Enter
until:1:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:2500
shot:01-boot1-first-setup

# Escape accepts First Setup's defaults. That is what applies keys_cursor.cfg
# (gArmagetron.cpp, sg_StartupPlayerMenu) -- the only moment in the program's
# life at which any keys_*.cfg is read.
key:Escape
until:2:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:3000
shot:02-boot1-after-first-setup
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot1-after-first-setup')

# ===========================================================================
# RELOAD 1. Everything after this mark is a new page load: new Module, new
# MEMFS, new mount, new populate.
# ===========================================================================
mark:RELOAD-1-REQUESTED
eval:location.reload()

until:2:90000:[PERSIST] populate ok
mark:BOOT-2-POPULATED
wait:1500

eval:(()=>{const S={};S.rd=(p)=>{try{return Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){return null}};S.line=(t,k)=>{if(t===null)return null;const m=t.split('\n').find(l=>{const s=l.trim();return s.indexOf(k)===0&&/\s/.test(s.charAt(k.length))});return m?m.trim().slice(k.length).trim().replace(/\s+/g,' '):null};S.keys=(t)=>t===null?[]:t.split('\n').filter(l=>l.trim().indexOf('KEYBOARD')===0).map(l=>l.trim().replace(/\s+/g,' '));S.binds=(K,a)=>K.filter(l=>l.indexOf(' PLAYER_BIND '+a+' 1')>0).map(l=>l.split(' ')[1]);S.dump=(phase)=>{const c=S.rd('/persist/var/user.cfg');const cv=document.getElementById('canvas');const K=S.keys(c);console.log('[MILE] '+JSON.stringify({phase:phase,canvas_w:cv.width,canvas_h:cv.height,present:c!==null,bytes:c===null?null:c.length,first_use:S.line(c,'FIRST_USE'),fullscreen:S.line(c,'FULLSCREEN'),screenmode:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE'),screenmode_w:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE_W'),screenmode_h:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE_H'),windowsize_w:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_WINDOWSIZE_W'),windowsize_h:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_WINDOWSIZE_H'),n_keyboard:K.length,left_binds:S.binds(K,'CYCLE_TURN_LEFT'),right_binds:S.binds(K,'CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT'),tip_left:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_LEFT_TOOLTIP'),tip_right:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT_TOOLTIP')}));return phase};window.__mile=S;return 'probe installed'})()

# ===========================================================================
# BOOT 2 -- the returning player. A page that has written nothing yet.
# ===========================================================================
#
# THE CANVAS IS 1024x768 HERE, from web/shell.html's own attributes, and the
# checker records it. That is the baseline A1's payoff is measured against.
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot2-before-play')

click:#start
wait:10000

# A2, ON THE SCREEN: the main menu, not the language menu.
shot:03-boot2-main-menu
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot2-after-boot')

# ------------------------------------------------- the player picks a size
# Main menu: Play Game / Player Setup / System Setup / About / Exit Game.
key:Down:2
wait:900
shot:04-boot2-cursor-on-system-setup
key:Enter
wait:1800
shot:05-boot2-system-setup

# Display Settings is the top item and already selected.
key:Enter
wait:1800
shot:06-boot2-display-settings

# Screen Mode is the top item and already selected.
key:Enter
wait:2500
shot:07-boot2-screen-mode

# Window Size is the top item; Screen Resolution is one row below it.
key:Down
wait:1200
shot:08-boot2-cursor-on-screen-resolution

mark:THE-PLAYER-PICKS-320x200
# Twenty Lefts against a list of fourteen, from the last entry. See the
# clamping note in the header: any count from thirteen up lands on 320x200.
key:Left:20
wait:1500
shot:09-boot2-resolution-now-320x200

# STILL IN THE MENU: the choice is in memory, the file has not been written,
# and -- the part that matters for A1 -- the CANVAS HAS NOT CHANGED. Nothing
# has called sr_ReinitDisplay.
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot2-chosen-menu-still-open')

mark:ESCAPE-LEAVES-SCREEN-MODE
key:Escape
until:3:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:1500
shot:10-boot2-back-in-display-settings

# The choice is now in the file. The canvas is still 1024x768.
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot2-after-menu-leave')

key:Escape
until:4:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:1200
key:Escape
until:5:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:1500
shot:11-boot2-back-at-main-menu

# ------------------------------------------------------- the player plays
# Up twice puts the cursor back on Play Game; Down once inside that submenu
# selects "Start New Game" (the submenu lists Network Game / Start New Game /
# Game Settings / Back, top to bottom).
key:Up:2
wait:900
key:Enter
wait:2000
shot:12-boot2-play-game-submenu
key:Down
wait:1200
shot:13-boot2-cursor-on-start-new-game
mark:START-NEW-GAME
key:Enter
wait:3000

# A2, IN THE LADDER LOG: a round starts. On a first-use boot this same key
# sequence does not reach a game at all -- it is the language menu, then First
# Setup -- so a NEW_ROUND here is a statement about which screen boot 2 came up
# on, not only about the file.
until:1:90000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-1-STARTED
wait:1200
shot:14-boot2-round1-driving

# A3. The arrow keys, and nothing else. Neither Left nor Right is bound to any
# other action in this configuration, and the only other keys this script
# presses in game are Escape (INGAME_MENU). Spread over three rounds because a
# press only counts while the cycle is alive, and the human dies to an AI in a
# few seconds; the counters need two lefts and three rights TOTAL, and
# uActionTooltip::Count is a no-op once a counter reaches zero, so the extra
# presses cost nothing and cannot overshoot.
mark:ROUND-1-STEERING
key:Left:2
key:Right:3
wait:1200
shot:15-boot2-round1-after-steering

until:2:120000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-2-STARTED
wait:1200
mark:ROUND-2-STEERING
key:Left:2
key:Right:3
wait:1200
shot:16-boot2-round2-after-steering

until:3:120000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-3-STARTED
wait:1200
mark:ROUND-3-STEERING
key:Left:2
key:Right:3
wait:1500
shot:17-boot2-round3-after-steering

# Escape opens the In-Game Menu (config/default.cfg binds keysym 27 to
# INGAME_MENU, and default.cfg is not loaded on this boot -- so this too only
# works because user.cfg carries the bind). Escape again closes it, which is a
# menu exit and therefore a save: that is how the decremented counters reach
# the file where this script can read them.
mark:INGAME-MENU-OPENED
key:Escape
wait:2500
shot:18-boot2-ingame-menu
key:Escape
until:6:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
mark:INGAME-MENU-LEFT
wait:1500
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot2-after-steering')

# ===========================================================================
# RELOAD 2.
# ===========================================================================
mark:RELOAD-2-REQUESTED
eval:location.reload()

until:3:90000:[PERSIST] populate ok
mark:BOOT-3-POPULATED
wait:1500

eval:(()=>{const S={};S.rd=(p)=>{try{return Module.FS.readFile(p,{encoding:'utf8'})}catch(e){return null}};S.line=(t,k)=>{if(t===null)return null;const m=t.split('\n').find(l=>{const s=l.trim();return s.indexOf(k)===0&&/\s/.test(s.charAt(k.length))});return m?m.trim().slice(k.length).trim().replace(/\s+/g,' '):null};S.keys=(t)=>t===null?[]:t.split('\n').filter(l=>l.trim().indexOf('KEYBOARD')===0).map(l=>l.trim().replace(/\s+/g,' '));S.binds=(K,a)=>K.filter(l=>l.indexOf(' PLAYER_BIND '+a+' 1')>0).map(l=>l.split(' ')[1]);S.dump=(phase)=>{const c=S.rd('/persist/var/user.cfg');const cv=document.getElementById('canvas');const K=S.keys(c);console.log('[MILE] '+JSON.stringify({phase:phase,canvas_w:cv.width,canvas_h:cv.height,present:c!==null,bytes:c===null?null:c.length,first_use:S.line(c,'FIRST_USE'),fullscreen:S.line(c,'FULLSCREEN'),screenmode:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE'),screenmode_w:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE_W'),screenmode_h:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_SCREENMODE_H'),windowsize_w:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_WINDOWSIZE_W'),windowsize_h:S.line(c,'ARMAGETRON_WINDOWSIZE_H'),n_keyboard:K.length,left_binds:S.binds(K,'CYCLE_TURN_LEFT'),right_binds:S.binds(K,'CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT'),tip_left:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_LEFT_TOOLTIP'),tip_right:S.line(c,'CYCLE_TURN_RIGHT_TOOLTIP')}));return phase};window.__mile=S;return 'probe installed'})()

# ===========================================================================
# BOOT 3 -- the payoff. A page that has written nothing.
# ===========================================================================
#
# A1, THE BEFORE HALF. The canvas is 1024x768: web/shell.html's attributes,
# untouched, because main() has not run. Whatever it is after the click cannot
# have come from the page.
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot3-before-play')
shot:19-boot3-before-play

click:#start
wait:10000

# A1, THE AFTER HALF, and A2 on screen at the same time: a 320x200 canvas
# showing the MAIN MENU.
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot3-after-boot')
shot:20-boot3-main-menu-at-320x200

# A2's strongest form. Enter opens the Play Game submenu; Escape leaves it,
# which saves. If this boot had re-run the first-use path it would have loaded
# config/default.cfg, which would have put CYCLE_TURN_LEFT_TOOLTIP back to
# "0 2 1 1 1" -- so a file that still reads 0 after a save the RUNNING PROGRAM
# performed is evidence about its memory, not about untouched bytes.
#
# Escape is pressed on the SUBMENU and never on the main menu: at the main menu
# Escape is Exit Game (gArmagetron.cpp tears the display down after MainMenu()
# returns), which would end the run.
key:Enter
wait:2000
shot:21-boot3-play-game-submenu
mark:ESCAPE-LEAVES-PLAY-GAME-SUBMENU
key:Escape
until:7:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
mark:BOOT-3-SAVED-FROM-MEMORY
wait:1500
eval:window.__mile.dump('boot3-after-save')
shot:22-boot3-final

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTROL: can this transcript see a browser-reported JS error at all? Last,
# because web/shell.html turns an uncaught error into a red failure banner.
# Without it, "no [EXCEPTION] in the run" is a silence, not an observation --
# M1's Firefox transcript was read as clean when it was merely deaf
# (docs/porting/browser-runtime-notes.md section 9).
mark:positive-control-deliberate-uncaught-error-follows
